# Rust conventions

The `[workspace.lints]` table and `cargo clippy -- --deny=warnings` in the
nox `rust` session own the mechanical rules: pedantic clippy; `unwrap_used`,
`expect_used`, `panic`, and `todo` as warnings; `unsafe_code` denied. This
file lists only what differs from defaults or is specific to this layout.

- `crates/{{ crate_name }}` is the library: no `pyo3`, testable with
  `cargo test`. `crates/{{ crate_name }}-py` is the binding: argument
  conversion and error mapping only, no logic. Its `///` comments are Python
  docstrings in Google style.
- Every function exported from the binding has a matching signature in
  `src/{{ package_name }}/_core.pyi`. Change them together. The binding is
  tested through pytest, not `cargo test`.
- Lint overrides are `#[expect(lint, reason = "...")]`, never `#[allow]`.
  An `expect("...")` is allowed only for an invariant documented on the
  same line.
- Errors: one `#[non_exhaustive]` enum per crate derived with `thiserror`;
  lowercase messages without a trailing period.
- Private by default; widen visibility one step at a time for a real
  caller. Fields stay private; public enums are `#[non_exhaustive]`.
- Spell words out in your own identifiers (`configuration`, `index`) except
  ecosystem conventions (`len`, `iter`, `fmt`).
- Don't fix borrow-checker errors with `.clone()`, `Rc<RefCell<_>>`, or
  `Arc<Mutex<_>>` unless you decided the ownership model on purpose and say
  so in a comment.
- Verify third-party APIs against the version in `Cargo.lock` before using
  them; guessed method names are the most common failure in agent-written
  Rust.
- Run `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` after every edit. `uv sync`
  rebuilds the extension after changes under `crates/`.
